NAME
    srv - manage the portable local lifecycle of a service

SYNOPSIS
    srv start <service>
    srv stop [-f] <service>
    srv host user install <service>
    srv host user uninstall <service>
    srv host user start <service>
    srv host user stop <service>
    srv host user restart <service>
    srv host system install <service> <account>
    srv host system uninstall <service>
    srv host system start <service>
    srv host system stop <service>
    srv host system restart <service>

DESCRIPTION
    srv provides the portable local service lifecycle owned by the technical m
    layer. The service process remains a foreground program from its own point of
    view; an internal native supervisor owns the child process for the managed
    lifecycle. The public srv command asks that supervisor to start or stop the
    service through a private local control socket.

    For a provider-backed service, <service> is a facility identity. srv resolves
    the configured system facility default to one installed concrete provider,
    validates that concrete's materialized facility-service start realization, and
    launches the exact package command belonging to that concrete through the active
    m bootstrap. This preserves m command dispatch and normal package-launcher
    semantics independently of the host nohup PATH. Consumer package bindings are
    not consulted. The normal package launcher remains responsible for package HOME,
    environment, and dependency preparation.

    Starting a service requires a configured system facility default and a valid
    installed service realization for the selected concrete provider. Command-name
    conventions and PATH are not consulted as an alternate service-discovery path.

COMMANDS
    start <service>
        Start the service if it is not already running under valid managed runtime
        state. Standard output and standard error from the service are appended to
        its managed service log.

    stop [-f] <service>
        Stop a managed running service with SIGTERM and wait a finite period for
        clean termination. If no active managed service remains, stop succeeds
        idempotently.

    host user install <service>
        Persist the service in the calling POSIX account's native user supervisor
        without starting it in the current session. Registration records the
        validated system profile active in the installing m context. Future starts
        and restarts use that profile even if the account defaults or another shell's
        context changes. The service must resolve through a valid system facility
        default/provider realization within the registered profile. Reinstall while
        the service is running updates later launches and does not retarget the
        running process. Host-account discovery follows the actual POSIX account
        rather than caller-overridden HOME/XDG state roots.

    host user uninstall <service>
        Stop/unload the hosted service when necessary and remove its persistent
        native registration.

    host user start <service>
        Validate the registered system profile against the installation inventory,
        then start the installed native user-supervisor registration. Current system
        facility-default intent is resolved within the registered profile for this
        launch. A missing, malformed or unmanaged profile fails closed.

    host user stop <service>
        Stop/unload the current hosted process while leaving the native registration
        installed.

    host user restart <service>
        Validate the registered system profile and restart the hosted service through
        the native supervisor, resolving current system facility-default intent within
        that profile for the replacement process.

    host system install <service> <account>
        Administratively reconcile a persistent system-supervisor registration for
        the service using an explicit pre-existing non-root POSIX account. The caller
        must already execute with effective UID 0; srv never invokes sudo/doas or
        creates/modifies the account. Install resolves and records the exact current
        concrete provider, prepares its service-specific system package HOME and
        enables boot persistence without starting the service.

    host system uninstall <service>
        Administratively stop/unload and remove native system registration. The
        execution account and authoritative package/service state are preserved.

    host system start <service>
        Start the installed system registration only when the current system
        facility default still resolves to the concrete provider reconciled by the
        last install.

    host system stop <service>
        Stop/unload the current system-hosted process while leaving persistent
        registration installed.

    host system restart <service>
        Restart/reload the installed system service only when provider selection
        still matches the reconciled concrete registration.

OPTIONS
    -f
        Apply only to stop. Bypass the normal caller-ownership check and explicitly
        request stopping the managed service. This is a lifecycle override, not an
        authentication or security boundary.

STATE
    srv resolves its user-scoped technical run and log roots through state-path:

        state-path user sys srv run
        state-path user sys srv log

    Runtime metadata below those roots is inspection/recovery data only. The
    running supervisor's direct-child relationship is the authority for a stop;
    persisted PID data never authorizes a signal. The supervisor is keyed by the
    installation root and effective POSIX UID, and uses an owner-only runtime
    directory under m_ROOT/state. Its local control socket accepts peers only
    from the same UID. The logical m user-state identity is not used as an
    account-security boundary.

    The C helper source is native/sys/srv-supervisor.c. A source checkout must build
    it before portable start/stop:

        ./tools/build-srv-supervisor.sh

    The build writes libexec/sys/srv-supervisor. The m bootstrap itself does not
    require a C compiler; an installed distribution must include the native
    helper built for its host. A clean checkout can build and verify a relocatable
    host distribution with ./tools/package-m-distribution.sh; the resulting
    dist/m-os-<osarch>.tar.gz contains the helper at that same path and records
    its source commit and target in M-DISTRIBUTION. The helper supports Linux
    and macOS.

    The supervisor keeps its direct service child unreaped until it has completed
    a stop request. It checks waitpid(WNOHANG) immediately before SIGTERM; an
    exiting child remains unreaped, so its PID cannot be reused for an unrelated
    process during the signal operation. Normal stop waits up to ten seconds and
    does not escalate to SIGKILL. The supervisor exits after one minute with no
    managed children.

    Each start request sends the calling process's environment as bounded,
    length-prefixed data over the private socket. The supervisor does not retain
    the first caller's environment and does not interpret environment values as
    shell source; each service starts with the environment of its own request.

    If the supervisor is killed or crashes, its service children may become
    orphaned. A later srv start/stop refuses to treat their persisted PID as
    authority and leaves both process and record untouched. The operator must
    identify and stop an orphan independently, then remove its stale runtime
    record before starting the service again. A graceful supervisor termination
    sends SIGTERM to children it still owns and reaps them before exit.

    Start resolves and records the selected concrete provider. Stop uses only
    the active supervisor's child relationship and does not resolve a provider
    again; later facility-default changes do not retarget the running instance.

HOST USER SUPERVISION
    On Linux, srv resolves the calling account's UID and home from host account
    data, connects to that account's systemd user manager through /run/user/<uid>,
    and persists a user unit below the account home at .config/systemd/user. The
    unit uses /bin/sh as the fixed
    ExecStart executable and carries the exact m bootstrap, srv command, internal
    foreground runner and service identity as separately serialized arguments.
    At registration, srv obtains its own physical command pathname from $0
    rather than depending on a command identity exported by the bootstrap. This
    avoids systemd executable-path restrictions for relocatable m roots
    containing spaces or metacharacters.

    On macOS, srv resolves the calling account's UID and home from host account
    data, uses that account's launchd gui/<uid> domain and persists a LaunchAgent
    below the account home at Library/LaunchAgents. ProgramArguments is constructed
    with
    plutil so the exact argv values remain data rather than interpolated XML/shell
    source.

    The host supervisor manages the provider foreground process directly. It never
    invokes portable srv start. The persistent registration does not freeze a
    provider concrete: each host start/restart resolves the current system facility
    default and provider service realization. User host integration does not enable
    systemd linger or an automatic restart policy.

HOST SYSTEM SUPERVISION
    System-host operations require pre-existing effective UID 0 administrative
    authority. The selected execution account must already exist and must be
    non-root. srv does not create/remove accounts and does not invoke privilege
    escalation itself.

    System registration records the exact concrete provider prepared by install.
    The system facility default remains authoritative selection intent: each hosted
    launch re-resolves it and fails as stale registration if it no longer matches.
    Re-run "srv host system install <service> <account>" to reconcile a changed
    provider before a later start/restart.

    The provider process runs through the ordinary package launcher with system
    package State Instance equal to the service identity. Its package HOME is
    therefore equivalent to:

        state-path system pkg <provider-package> home <service>

    The service account owns only that prepared mutable HOME. srv does not grant it
    ownership or write access to m/product/package executable roots. Those roots must
    already be readable/executable by the selected account under host permissions.

    Linux installs a systemd system unit with Type=simple and User=<account>.
    macOS installs a LaunchDaemon under /Library/LaunchDaemons with
    UserName=<account>. Both supervise the foreground provider directly, preserve
    exact m/srv argv as data, and do not add an automatic restart policy.

READINESS
    Successful start establishes that the launched process survived the initial
    launch check. It does not establish application-specific readiness or health.

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires a nonempty m_ROOT inherited from an initialized m environment.
    From an uninitialized host shell, invoke m srv [arguments].


EXIT STATUS
    0     The requested lifecycle operation completed successfully. This includes
          idempotent stop cases where the service is not running.
    1     A general lifecycle or host operation failed.
    2     The invocation, host scope/action, or service name is invalid.
    4     The native supervisor helper is unavailable.
    5     The supervisor request failed.
    6     The supervisor returned an invalid response.
    7     Start refused orphaned runtime state.
    8     The service failed during startup.
    9     Stop refused orphaned runtime state.
    10    Stop timed out; the supervisor still owns the service process.

EXAMPLES
    Start a service named web:

        srv start web

    Stop it normally:

        srv stop web

    Explicitly stop it despite caller-ownership metadata:

        srv stop -f web

    Install and start it under the calling account's native host supervisor:

        srv host user install web
        srv host user start web

    Reconcile and start it as a system service using a pre-existing non-root
    account (the shell must already have administrative/root authority):

        srv host system install web daemon
        srv host system start web

